# Deep-link app paths. When the OS does not hand an https link to the installed
# app, the browser lands here and this interstitial reconstructs the
# alexandria:// URL. Status 200 keeps the original URL visible.
#
# These live here rather than in netlify.toml because Nitro's netlify-static
# preset appends its own catch-all to this file:
#
#   /* /404.html 404
#
# Netlify evaluates _redirects before netlify.toml, so rules kept only in the
# toml lost to that catch-all and every one of these paths returned a 404.
# Order within this file is what makes them work — they must precede it.
/guardian/*   /app-open.html   200
/course/*     /app-open.html   200
/classroom/*  /app-open.html   200
/open         /app-open.html   200

# www -> apex. There is no DNS record for www.alexandria.ifftu.dev today, so
# anything typed or shared with the www prefix simply fails to resolve — no
# server is reached and no redirect can run. That includes link unfurlers: a
# social crawler handed the www form gets nothing back and renders no card.
#
# This rule is therefore INERT until the hostname is added as a domain alias in
# Netlify. It is here so that the moment it is, www 301s to the apex rather than
# serving the same pages under a second hostname — which would split the
# canonical, exactly the contradiction the IFFTU site had in reverse.
#
# Host-level rules need the full origin on the left; a bare /* would match the
# apex too and loop. The trailing ! forces the rule even where a static file
# would otherwise be served.
https://www.alexandria.ifftu.dev/*  https://alexandria.ifftu.dev/:splat  301!

# The old card was a 200 KB PNG carrying the previous site's design and copy
# ("decentralized", "blockchain-verified credentials"). Anything that already
# scraped that URL should land on the current card rather than a 404.
/og-image.png  /og/home.jpg  301

# /recruiter became /employers: the buyer is often a hiring manager or workforce
# programme, not a recruiter. Permanent, so existing links and any search equity
# follow rather than break.
/recruiter     /employers   301
/recruiter/*   /employers   301

# /developers became /technology. The old page was a short stack summary aimed at
# contributors; the new one explains how every part works and absorbs it, so the
# old path would otherwise be a thinner version of a page that already exists.
/developers    /technology   301
/developers/*  /technology   301
